  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 352 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nA clipper like system will push through the state today. This\nwill cool temperatures to near normal with highs in the mid 70s\nto mid 80s. Cloud cover in southern Canada will push into the\nnorthern half of North Dakota today. This could bring a few\nshowers as well to these areas. Weak instability may also\nprovide for an isolated thunderstorm this afternoon, although \nthe threat for severe weather is low. Increased northwest winds \nwill also be found as this clipper system moves through. It \nappears the winds will fall just short of advisory criteria, \nalthough gusts near 40 mph are possible this afternoon. There \nstill could be some near critical fire weather conditions across\nareas along, west, and south of the Missouri River and \nespecially the far southwest. These areas will see afternoon RH \nvalues near 30 percent with winds sustained at around 20 mph. \nThe stronger winds today are expected more in the north and \neast, and RH values should be improved today with the lower \ntemperatures. Cloud cover will also be an area of uncertainty \nfor near critical fire weather conditions. Thus will just \nmessage the limited near critical fire weather conditions in the\nAFD and the FWF for now. This system looks to push eastward \ntonight, bringing clearing skies and lessening winds to the \narea. Look for lows in the upper 40s to upper 50s as a result. \nDepending on timing of clearing skies and light winds, perhaps \nsome patchy fog is possible tonight into Wednesday morning, \nalthough dry air at the surface may limit this potential. \n\nSurface high on Wednesday, followed by a ridge aloft on\nThursday, will bring generally dry conditions for the mid week \nperiod. Look for high temperatures to generally remain near \nnormal in the mid 70s to mid 80s, although Thursday could see a \nslight warming trend in the upper 70s to upper 80s. Afternoon RH\nvalues will lower into the 20s and 30s each day, although winds\nshould generally be light except for some increased southerly \nwinds across the west on Thursday. The mentioned ridge could \nthen break down sometime Thursday night or Friday. The resultant\nsouthwest flow could bring slightly warmer temperatures and \nperhaps some more active weather. Chances for showers and \nthunderstorms look to return each day Friday through the \nweekend. CSU-MLP also showing at least an isolated severe \nweather threat possible each day through the weekend as well. \nBreezy winds may be found on Friday and again on Sunday. The \nbreezier winds do not line up with the lowest RH on Friday, \nwhich should help limit the fire weather concerns. Sunday could \nsee these breezier winds line up with some lower RH in the west,\nwhich may be worth monitoring for at least some increased fire \nweather concerns.",
